FEASIBILITY ARITHMETIC. I recomputed every size and speed estimate in /Users/kevinliu/repos/cotcodec-wt-localmoe/program/evidence/2026-10-16/local-frontier-moe-sweep/DOSSIER.md and did not edit it.

Primary inputs I measured or read myself on 2026-10-15:
- **GGUF tensor tables.** I read the header-only tensor tables of the actual files through HTTP range requests, with no tensor data downloaded. Files: Kimi K2.6 UD-Q2_K_XL, K2-Thinking UD-TQ1_0, antirez V4 Flash IQ2XXS (ds4 Q2), Unsloth V4 Flash UD-IQ3_XXS, Qwen3-235B-2507 UD-Q2_K_XL and UD-Q3_K_XL, DeepSeek-R1 UD-IQ1_S and UD-Q2_K_XL, GLM-4.7 UD-TQ1_0, and ggml-org gpt-oss-120b MXFP4. Read in full. Parser and outputs are in the scratchpad (tools/ggufhdr.py, hdr/*.json).
- **File sizes.** Hugging Face API tree listings (https://huggingface.co/api/models/<repo>/tree/main/<folder>), queried 2026-10-15, read in full.
- **Model configs.** Kimi K2 config.json (https://huggingface.co/moonshotai/Kimi-K2-Instruct, fields read) and DeepSeek-V4-Flash config.json (Unsloth mirror, read in full).
- **M5 Max memory bandwidth.** Apple MacBook Pro specs (https://www.apple.com/macbook-pro/specs/, skimmed): 614 GB/s for the 40-core GPU; 128 GB requires the 40-core GPU.
- **M5 Max SSD speed.** Apple newsroom, 2026-03-03 (https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/03/apple-introduces-macbook-pro-with-all-new-m5-pro-and-m5-max/, footnotes read): "up to 14.5GB/s", measured on an 8 TB preproduction unit with FIO, 1024 KB requests and IO depth 8. Reviewer figure of 13.6 GB/s on 4 TB units from https://macdailynews.com/2026/03/10/apples-new-m5-max-macbook-pro-delivers-a-huge-ssd-read-write-speed-boost/ (search summary only). No 2 TB M5 Max test found.
- **Local measurements on this Mac.** `sysctl hw.memsize` = 137,438,953,472 B (128 GiB). `headroom status`: 55.0 GB free (43%), swap 8.4 GB, memory-pressure warning.
- **Other sources:**
  - arXiv 2509.25689, full text via orx;
  - ds4 PERFORMANCE.md, skimmed (https://github.com/antirez/ds4/blob/main/docs/PERFORMANCE.md);
  - antirez/deepseek-v4.1-flash-gguf README, skimmed;
  - flash-moe README, skimmed (https://github.com/danveloper/flash-moe);
  - SGLang docs on KV duplication under TP, search summary only;
  - nvidia-smi showing 81559MiB per H100, search summary only (https://www.icc-usa.com/blog/hgx-8x-h100-benchmarks).

problems.claim:
A2 and section 1:
- 'Apple-GPU decoders achieve about 25-61%';
- 'I use e = 0.35-0.46 (215-282 GB/s)';
- F1: 'The measured 39 implies e ≈ 0.25-0.36'.

problems.evidence:
Implied bandwidth = tokens/s × bytes per token:
- **V4 Flash ds4, 2K context:** 39.35 tok/s [S95, recomputed] × 9.568 GB = 376.5 GB/s, e = 0.613.
- **gpt-oss-120b, llama.cpp:** 105 × 3.594 = 377 GB/s, e = 0.61.
- **gpt-oss-120b, MLX:** 79.7 × 3.594 = 286 GB/s, e = 0.47.
- **V4 Flash at longer context:** e = 0.54 at 32K and 0.43 at 64K, because KV and attention work grow.
- **Kimi DQ3_K_M on M3 Ultra:** with an Unsloth-like spine of about 8.5 GB, about 18 GB per token × 21.7 = about 390 GB/s, e ≈ 0.48, not 0.38.

Corrected short-context decode with e = 0.47-0.61 (288-377 GB/s):
- F1: 30-39 tok/s (dossier 54-71; measured 39.35);
- F2: 31-41 (dossier 46-60);
- O1: 80-105 (dossier 61-79);
- Q1: 29-38 (dossier 26-34);
- Q2: 27-35 (dossier 22-29). The community report of about 30 tok/s on a 546 GB/s M4 Max implies e ≈ 0.6, consistent with this;
- D4: 20-29 (dossier 24-43);
- K6(i) with a realistic 6.5-bpw spine (15.7 GB): 18-24.

problems.claim:
Streamed decode estimates:
- K1: 1.0-2.7;
- K2: 2.5-7.1, '88 GB = 26% of the 336 GB pool';
- K3: 4.0-12.2;
- D2: 2.4-7.4, 'routed share = 2/3 of active, pool ≈ 200 GB';
- D3: 6-16;
- Q5: 4.6-12.7;
- section 1: 'about 2-7 tokens/s'.

problems.evidence:
`headroom status` on 2026-10-15 (local measurement):
- Free: 43% (55.0 GB of 128 GB);
- Claude: 63.1 GB in 345 processes;
- Chrome: 24.2 GB; dev servers: 15.6 and 7.3 GB; model runs: 6.5-8.7 GB each;
- Swap: 8.4 GB used, 62.5 GB compressed, 'pressure warning'.

At this load, a streamer gets about 55 − 9.3 (non-routed weights) − 4.3 (KV at 32K) − 3 (buffers) ≈ 38 GB of cache. That is about 11% of the 331 GB K2.6 Q2 pool, so h ≈ 0.45-0.55, giving K2 decode of **1.4-3.0 tok/s**. Best case 0.45×6.9/10.6 + 15.42/377 = 0.334 s. Worst case 0.55×6.9/5.85 + 15.42/288 = 0.702 s.

From GGUF metadata, F16 KV:
- **Qwen3-235B** (94 layers × 4 KV heads × 128 × 2 × 2 B) = 192,512 B per token: 5.9 GiB at 32K, 11.75 GiB at 64K, 23.5 GiB at 128K. That is 6.3 GB read per token at 32K.
- **GLM-4.7** (92 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 × 2 × 2 B) = 376,832 B per token: 11.5 GiB at 32K, 23 GiB at 64K. That is 12.35 GB read per token at 32K.

Decode at e = 288-377 GB/s, short context and then 32K:
- Q1: 29-38, then **18-23**;
- Q2: 27-35, then **17-22**;
- G1 (GLM TQ1_0, about 11.9 GB per token): 24-32, then **12-16**. The dossier gives 23-60.

Fit limits:
- Q2 (97.0 GiB): Tight only up to about 41K tokens;
- GLM TQ1_0 (78.7 GiB): Yes up to about 32K, Tight at 64K;
- GLM IQ1_S (90.5 GiB): Tight up to about 40K, No beyond;
- G2 218B at 3.5 bits (88.8 GiB): 103.3 GiB at 32K (Tight), No at 64K.

Same assumptions (h 0.75, 13.6 GB/s, e 0.40) with measured bytes (routed 6.90, spine 8.53):
- top-8: 0.25×6.90/13.6 + 15.42/245.6 = 0.190 s → 5.3;
- top-6: 6.6 tok/s (**+26%**);
- top-4: 8.9 tok/s (**+69%**).
At 10.6 GB/s the gains are +27% and +73%.

